now if you've been following the news  recently you might remember this from  sagittar java just yesterday  the uk health security agency estimates  that the current rate of date the  current number of daily infections are  around 200  000. two hundred thousand  daily infections it's a really striking  number we had it on the news last night  so did lots of the newspapers actually  have a look at this front page of the  times you can see there it is right at  the front two hundred thousand daily uh  omicron infections same thing if you  looked at the front of the guardians so  this really was big news uh even  actually the ft uh had it on their front  page very serious newspaper two hundred  thousand omicron infections daily  it begs a question doesn't it where is  this number actually coming from is it  new intelligence privileged information  that the ukhs say the health security  agency has or is it something else  well 24 hours on i think i've worked it  out it is actually a back of an envelope  some that 200 000 comes from something  i'm going to show you right now which  any of us could really have worked out  and it begins with this okay i'm going  to show you exactly how they did it this  is case numbers so that's just your  number the one we see every day of daily  cases uh that's a seven day average and  what you'll probably remember is there's  a difference between cases positive  tests tests coming back positive  and infections infections is a kind of  modeled number where you're working out  how many people across the population  have covered at any given time in this  case this is new infections each day and  what you can see and what will be pretty  obvious to you is that there's a  difference see that blue line the case  is on the 21st of november it was about  35 000 on the same day infection's about  78 thousand so that is always higher  because you've got people who who aren't  getting tested basically and so what the  uk hsa did is they took that 78 000  number and they  kind of presumed and this is fine that  it was the same in early december as it  was in late november because we don't  have that data yet 78 thousand  then they went to the fact that we can't  we know that at that time broadly about  20 of the sequence cases were coming  back as omicron so they assumed at that  point  20 just over 20  that worked out as that dot over there  you can just about see it the red dots  23 000 omicron infections on the 7th of  december so 7th of december last week  but how do you get from that 23 000 to  that scary 200 000 number well let me  show you so we'll take this there's that  same dot and what we're going to do is  just double it we're doubling it every  two days actually just under every two  days every 1.9 days and you get that  line doubling every 1.9 days you get  that exponential increase you can see  the curve you'll be familiar with it  from all of the covert uh period when  you've been looking at those charts and  if you follow that line lo and behold on  the 13th of december 207 000 omicron  infections new infections on that day so  it is right according to this but it is  a back of an envelope uh some but it is  at that kind of level by the way that's  england it's not the uk as a whole 200  000 for england if you're talking about  uk as a whole you're probably up to  about 250 000 maybe even more  but what happens next if you follow that  line if you follow that logic then right  now we've got 300 000. so today one day  on three hundred thousand and by the end  of the week by saturday you're talking  about an extra 1.3 million on saturday  new daily cases on saturday now already  200 000 is the highest we have ever seen  if indeed it is true 1.3 million would  be just crazy numbers and by christmas  day  25th of december we can't even fit it in  here because it's so high up there it's  kind of well above the roof of the  studio you're talking about 17  million omicron infections on that day  and just bear in mind that's on that day  if you were to work out all of the kind  of cumulative infections you've had over  this period well  everyone in england would have it 55  million people in england would have it  just on the basis on the logic of this  line that the health secretary was  basing that 200 000 figure on it doesn't  mean it's invalid you know there is  definitely cause and use for these  things when we don't have the data to  back it up but nonetheless it's worth  just being clear where that number  actually came from it's also worth being  clear if you take a different assumption  about how fast you're doubling uh those  case numbers or those infection numbers  rather you get very different results so  just recently just last week the ukhsa  was talking about maybe cases uh  doubling where infections doubling about  2.5 days so that's at a slower rate and  2.5 days gives you this line still very  fast increase but nowhere near looking  like we that red line so by yesterday  the day that saturday was talking about  200 000 actually 120 000 omicron cases  not quite a record but certainly high  levels and by christmas well 3.4 million  on the day which is certainly very high  indeed certainly seems implausible but  nowhere near 17 million nowhere near  everyone in england  having had uh omicron but you know  laughing at this and to some extent it  is a reduction absurdum uh is to  undermine the fact that really there is  a serious point here which is that cases  are going up they are unlikely to go up  as half as fast as that or certainly  they're unlikely to continue going up as  fast as that in south africa we know  that they did go up at that rate for a  while and then they started to slow so  then it was kind of right now as far as  i can tell it's doubling maybe nine uh  every nine every ten days but when you  look at the actual data about what's  happening certainly the figures are  rising not quite as fast as that but  certainly rising these dots here just  showing you those case numbers across  the uk so actual cases that we're  detecting and the latest figure 59 610  new cases close to a daily record and if  you just look at the kind of seven day  average well look at that black line it  is going up and i think pretty soon  maybe by the end of the week certainly  by next week we're likely to see a  record number  of cases  but here's the thing as we should know  this already but it's worth just  underlining it it's not really all about  that line it's about this line as well  this is admissions for to hospitals and  what you can probably make out is those  two lines very correlated uh in those  early parts of the cro of the pandemic  you can see at the start there we didn't  have much testing but emissions were  high but look at that second wave you  can see those lines really correlated  with each other and look at what's  happened since doing kind of as a result  in part of the vaccination program you  can see admissions very low while cases  are high and so the real thing we're  going to have to focus on is not just  that black line or indeed the line that  the health secretary was talking about  about infections but also what's going  on with emissions what's going on in  healthcare and if there's one part of  the country right now which is worth  focusing on especially  it's london because you've got a really  high increase in london just look at  that line uh for cases the black line  for cases in london at the moment nearly  vertical going up very fast there is a  lot of omicron uh in london that seems  to be the the epicenter but still  admissions is going to be the big  question  so keeping an eye on that red line and  working out whether it's going to go up  that is the thing that we're going to  have to be doing over the course of the  next few weeks

